{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target proposes 'Shared Mechanism' but defines the mechanism too broadly to be substantive. It claims both sides rely on analyzing the 'pragmatic decision to act.' However, the accusation of 'smuggling induction' (SRC_005) is typically a logical critique asserting that a conclusion violates formal rules of inference regardless of the agent's intent or decision process. By reducing a formal logical objection to a mere 'pragmatic decision,' the target mischaracterizes the nature of the accusation. The 'Refuted If' condition admits this by suggesting the accusation could derive from 'logical syntax' alone; if so, the proposed relation fails because the two endpoints do not share the same mechanism—one is pragmatic/decision-based, the other is syntactic/logical.","successor_question":"If the accusation of smuggling induction rests solely on logical syntax as admitted in the 'Refuted If' clause, how can the relation validly claim a shared mechanism of 'pragmatic decision'?","target":"690fd85f319fbf001a08bbb905eafd1c59b8923ec88e099ffc072c8d455b6668"}]}